"link.pcp.claim-source-13"],"id":"pcp.claim-source-13","kind":"claim","label":"The reverse inclusion","locator":"The reverse inclusion, Section 7","source_id":"paper","statement":"For the converse, take the verifier and bounds $p,\\alpha,\\beta$ of Theorem 1.1, guess the first $p(n)$ proof bits and simulate the verifier, in polynomial time, on each of the at most $2^\\beta(2(n+2))^\\alpha$ random strings. Perfect completeness gives a prefix accepted on every run. If such a prefix existed for a no-instance, its zero extension would have acceptance probability one, contradicting soundness. The resulting witness relation is decidable in polynomial time, so the language lies in NP.","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/nodes/pcp.claim-source-13","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/nodes/pcp.claim-source-13","id":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/edges/%5B%22pcp.claim-source-13%22%2C%22lean.PCP.pcp_subset_np_concrete%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/edges/%5B%22pcp.claim-source-13%22%2C%22lean.PCP.pcp_subset_np_concrete%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","semantic_class":"formalization_correspondence","to":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/nodes/lean.PCP.pcp_subset_np_concrete"},{"derived":true,"family":"provenance","from":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/nodes/pcp.claim-source-13","id":"field:2374cc5c94d0eee70c6a3a62cfcb8efc94ed52d36a18b7848858475eaffbc6bf","kind":"citation_ids","owner_ref":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b0ba8dc80a1f381430efc8a71213a44d/nodes/pcp.claim-source-13","semantic_class":"provenance_field_reference","source_field":"citation_ids","to":"sha256:0544eb2e356b797914aa8df20ae92a44b